{#-
    Timestamps for the seed data, relative to when it is rendered.

    Plain Jinja2 plus the two things Ansible provides out of the box:
    the now() global and the strftime filter.  No filter plugin needed.
    lab/instance.py supplies the same two with the same calling
    convention, so these files render identically in the proof suite.

    The seed only works for a while after it is rendered (about three
    weeks -- see README), so render it when the data is *loaded*, not
    on every run of a playbook.
-#}
{#-
    Note now(), not now(utc=true): Ansible's UTC variant returns a naive
    datetime, which .timestamp() then reads as local time, shifting every
    value by the controller's UTC offset.  Plain now() is naive local
    time, which .timestamp() handles correctly; strftime(..., utc=true)
    does the formatting in UTC.
-#}
{%- macro ago(days=0, hours=0, minutes=0) -%}
{{ '%Y%m%d%H%M%SZ' | strftime(now().timestamp() - ((days * 24 + hours) * 60 + minutes) * 60, utc=true) }}
{%- endmacro -%}
{%- macro ahead(days=0, hours=0, minutes=0) -%}
{{ ago(days=-days, hours=-hours, minutes=-minutes) }}
{%- endmacro -%}
